# Optics 21 — Fraunhofer diffraction by a circular aperture: the Airy pattern
#
# The Fraunhofer pattern of a circular aperture of diameter D is
#
#     I(θ) / I₀ = (2 J₁(x) / x)²,   x = π D sin θ / λ,
#
# with the Bessel function J₁(x) = (1/π) ∫₀^π cos(τ − x sin τ) dτ.
#
# (a) Find the first dark ring (the first zero of J₁) and the Rayleigh
#     criterion sin θ = 1.2197 λ/D.
# (b) Find the half-intensity point (the FWHM of the central peak) and the
#     first bright ring (dI/dx = 0) with its intensity (textbook: x = 5.1356,
#     I/I₀ = 0.0175).
# (c) What fraction of the light falls inside the first dark ring? Integrate
#     the pattern, I(x) x dx, and compare with Rayleigh's 1 − J₀² − J₁² = 83.8 %.
# (d) The Hubble Space Telescope: D = 2.40 m, focal length f = 57.6 m, at
#     λ = 550 nm. Find the angular resolution (in milliarcseconds) and the
#     radius of the Airy disk in the focal plane.
# (Hecht, Optics §10.2.5; Born & Wolf §8.5.2)

J0(x) = (1/π) ∫ cos(x sin(τ)) dτ from 0 to π
J1(x) = (1/π) ∫ cos(τ - x sin(τ)) dτ from 0 to π
I(x) = (2 J1(x) / x)²

# (a) the first zero of J₁ (x = 0 is not a zero of the pattern, so start at 3)
solve J1(x1) = 0 for x1 from 3 to 5
print "(a) first dark ring at x =", x1 to 10 digits, "  sin θ D/λ =", x1 / π to 10 digits

# (b) half maximum, and the first bright ring
solve I(x_half) = ½ for x_half from 0.5 to 3
print "(b) half intensity at x =", x_half to 10 digits
dI = I'
solve dI(x_ring) = 0 for x_ring from 4 to 7
print "    first bright ring at x =", x_ring to 10 digits, "  I/I0 =", I(x_ring) to 10 digits

# (c) the encircled energy: ∫₀^∞ I(x) x dx = 2
inside = ½ ∫ I(x) x dx from 0 to x1
print "(c) fraction inside the first dark ring =", inside to 10 digits,
      "  1 - J0² - J1² =", 1 - J0(x1)² - J1(x1)² to 10 digits

# (d) Hubble
D = 2.40 m
f_len = 57.6 m
λ = 550 nm
θ_R = asin(x1 λ / (π D))
print "(d) Rayleigh angle =", θ_R in mas to 8 digits
print "    FWHM =", 2 asin(x_half λ / (π D)) in mas to 8 digits
print "    Airy radius in the focal plane =", f_len tan(θ_R) in μm to 8 digits
